      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Chest pain in patients with hypertrophic cardiomyopathy seems to be caused by relative myocardial ischemia due to the left ventricular outflow pressure gradient and myocardial hypertrophy. However, in 2 cases of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy chest pain was associated with coronary vasospasm. Thus, chest pain in these cases was decreased not by a beta-blocker but by isosorbide dinitrate and a calcium antagonist. Because beta-blockers are commonly used for hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy and chest pain may be aggravated by beta-blockers in patients with coronary vasospasm, a combination of beta-blocker, isosorbide dinitrate and calcium antagonist was necessary for this hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with variant angina."},
